1.6.m:
The Sūta said:
The waters indeed arose from fire when the earth was destroyed by fire;
when all that moves and is still had vanished into this single intermediate space.

1.8.m:
The realm of the seven seers is handed down for the sky-dwellers;
the realm of Prajāpati is for householders, and the abode of Brahmā is for renunciants;
immortal is the realm of the yogins, [whereas] for those without master or rule, none exists.

appearing in various states—as gods, asuras, and ancestors, as cattle, birds, and reptiles,
as trees, denizens of hell, and insects;
and for the sake of the masterless creatures, he fashioned them from himself.

1.9.m:
Thinking of them as fathers, the Lord meditated on those sons;
he created the ancestors from his two flanks, in the interval between night and day;
therefore those divine beings are ancestors, and in them lies that quality of being sons.

immediately night and day, twilight, and moonlight were born.
Moonlight, twilight, and day consist purely of sattva by nature;
night consists purely of tamas, and therefore it has three watches.

Therefore the gods, radiant from the essence of shining, were seen created from his mouth;
because their birth is by day, they are strong by day.

